SECTION 4 ROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES                                                                                                 9


The responsibilities of a biosafety officer could include advising on and/or contributing
to:


n biosafety, biosecurity and technical consultations;


n apprising senior management and/or the biosafety committee of safety-related
  issues within the organization;


n ensuring risk assessments and authorizations for work are in place;


n developing and/or delivering safety-related training activities;


n periodic laboratory inspections, audits and assessments;


n reporting, investigating and following up on accidents or biosafety incidents;


n developing and maintaining the biosafety manual and standard operating
  procedures (SOPs) related to biosafety and biosecurity;


n promoting and monitoring compliance with legislative or regulatory requirements;
  and


n communicating with personnel at all levels of the organization about biosafety.


4 . 4 Laborator  y personnel

The laboratory director or manager is responsible for implementing processes within
the facility or laboratory that comply with the organization’s biosafety policies. They
may also have the following responsibilities:


n being aware of and complying with the organization’s biosafety policies and
  procedures;


n ensuring that adequate and timely resources are made available for the elements
  of the biosafety programme (for example, performing risk assessments, attending
  training);


n working closely with biosafety personnel to ensure that appropriate risk control
  strategies are implemented;


n contributing to the development of facility- or laboratory-specific SOPs;
